NEW[66] = ("Because the threshold also decides which samples enter, the two effects were separated. On the samples retained at 3,000 reads, matrices recomputed at 500 reads were nearly invariant for the abundance-weighted metrics (weighted UniFrac r = 0.9994, Bray-Curtis r = 0.9985) but not for the presence-based ones (Jaccard r = 0.9617, unweighted UniFrac r = 0.9373), observed richness falling by 42 %. Retention at 3,000 reads is not random with respect to category: in the caudal fin 50 % of intermediate hybrids passed the threshold against 86 % of P. toxostoma (pass 1), and 67 % of all 42 hybrids (pass 2), a gap of 36 and 20 points respectively. Claims resting on abundance-weighted metrics are therefore accompanied by a sensitivity analysis at 500 reads, where the caudal-fin gap falls to 3.8 points (pass 1) and 5.7 points (pass 2) and 2,067 samples are retained (1,825 in pass 1); claims resting on presence-based metrics are reported at 3,000 reads with the selection declared, the midgut gap (14.0 and 8.9 points) persisting at 500 reads in any case (Supplementary Table S6 and Figure S2).")
NEW[68] = ("Community composition was analysed by PERMANOVA [45] on the averaged dissimilarity matrices, stratified by tissue, a stratification imposed by the design: 168 of the 180 fish occupy the same plate column across all four tissues, so that plate position is not estimable in a pooled model carrying fish identity. Station, not site code, is the spatial unit, since two site codes each span two Suran stations that share no genotypic category; sequencing run is nested within library preparation (§8.8), and every analysis of category is run in the two passes of §1. Because genotypic category, station and plate position are mutually entangled (Results), each model is fitted in both sequential orderings and the interval between them is reported, the ordering that serves category first bounding it from above and the ordering that serves it last from below. Plate column is not entered in the principal models, but every model testing category is refitted with it as a covariate and reported as a sensitivity analysis. Permutations are constrained within station blocks where the question concerns a within-station contrast. Dispersion was tested with PERMDISP [46], since the transgressive hypothesis concerns the variability of hybrid microbiota and PERMANOVA is itself sensitive to heterogeneous dispersion; distances to group medians were corrected for small-sample bias (bias.adjust = TRUE [48]), hybrids being the smallest category in every stratum. Both analyses were run with vegan 2.7-5 [47]. Four dissimilarities — Bray–Curtis, Jaccard, unweighted and weighted UniFrac — and four alpha indices — observed richness, Shannon, inverse Simpson and Faith's phylogenetic diversity — are reported per tissue. Because resequencing one library recovers only 48 % of the ASVs present at 1–3 reads (Results), a result is stated as established only where at least one abundance-weighted and one presence-based metric support it, and is otherwise reported as metric-dependent with the supporting metrics named; the rule is applied metric by metric, since Bray–Curtis patterns with the two presence-based dissimilarities whereas weighted UniFrac stands apart. Running text quotes Shannon and Bray–Curtis, the remaining metrics being reported alongside.")
NEW[74] = ("Alpha diversity was summarised per individual and tissue as the mean over the three runs of the four rarefied indices, analysed on the log scale. First, external (caudal fin, gill) and internal (midgut, hindgut) compartments, and midgut and hindgut, were contrasted within individual by paired t-tests on log values, with Wilcoxon signed-rank tests alongside, and expressed as ratios of geometric means with 95 % confidence intervals. Second, the variance of log alpha diversity over the 1,784 rarefied samples was partitioned by a linear model with tissue, site-year, individual, library preparation and sequencing run, reporting sequential and marginal shares. Third, genotypic category was tested per tissue and pass by ordinary least squares, log(index) ~ station + category, with an F test of the category term and the contrasts hybrid − C. nasus, hybrid − P. toxostoma and hybrid − parental midpoint with 95 % confidence intervals; plate position was added as a covariate in a sensitivity model. Hybrids were classified as intermediate when their estimate lay between the two parental estimates and did not differ from their midpoint; as dominant when their interval included one parent and excluded the other; as transgressive when their estimate lay beyond the parental range with an interval excluding the nearer parent; and as undetermined when no criterion or more than one was met. This rule, and the controls of §8.12, were declared before computation.")
NEW[76] = ("The 4H index [14] was computed with the R package HybridMicrobiomes 0.1.1 [49] and phyloseq [50] on three host classes — C. nasus, hybrids and P. toxostoma — in each pass, tissue and sequencing run. Its parameters and reading rules were fixed in a document committed before any index was computed: core fraction ρ = 0.5 at genus rank, with sensitivity at ρ = 0.3 and 0.7 and at family rank, and no abundance threshold (ϑ = ε = 0); ρ ≥ 0.8 was excluded because the Intersection dimension vanishes there on the published natural hybrid system. Counts were aggregated to genus — 48.6 % of ASVs, carrying 64.1 % of reads, were assigned to a genus (family: 77.9 % and 90.6 %) — and unassigned ASVs were excluded. Samples carrying at least 1,000 genus-assigned reads (1,749 of the 1,784 rarefied samples) were rarefied to that depth at each of 500 bootstraps; on the published natural hybrid system the parental axis varies by at most 0.067 between 1,000 and 10,000 reads [14]. Each bootstrap drew without replacement the same number of hosts N from each class, N being one less than the smallest class of pass 1 in the stratum (9 in the caudal fin, 10–19 elsewhere), and the same N was applied to pass 2; pass 2 was also computed at its own N. The abundance-based version of the index, the pre-analysis of core fractions, the null plane and a null hybrid averaging one randomly drawn individual of each parental species were computed with the same settings. An axis — parental (Union + Intersection) or transgressive (Gain + Loss) — was called dominant when it exceeded 0.5 in all three runs, and robust when the same axis also dominated at ρ = 0.3, at ρ = 0.7, at family rank and in the abundance-based version; two compartments were called different when the 95 % bootstrap intervals of the transgressive axis were disjoint in all three runs. The PERMANOVA comparison of bootstrap sets offered by the package was not used, because its p-value decreases with the arbitrarily chosen number of bootstraps. Preferential retention of the taxa shared by both parents was inferred when more than 95 % of bootstraps lay on the Intersection side of the null plane in all three runs.")
NEW[81] = ("Raw sequence data have been deposited in the European Nucleotide Archive (ENA) under accession PRJEB124417: 768 samples and 2,304 sequencing runs (4,608 FASTQ files, 11.7 GB), with run titles encoding the nested replication structure of §8.8, sample metadata following the GSC MIxS host-associated checklist (ERC000013), and controls, mock aliquots and empty wells included and identified as such. Per-run accessions, file names and MD5 checksums are given in Supplementary Table S7.")
