# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- Results
NEW[91] = ("The two runs sharing a library were markedly more similar to each other (mean Bray–Curtis 0.118, Jaccard 0.445) than either was to the run from the independent library (0.248 and 0.720 respectively; 548 samples reaching 3,000 reads in all three runs, §8.8); the two library-crossing comparisons were statistically indistinguishable from each other (paired Wilcoxon, p = 0.48) and both differed from the within-library comparison (p < 10⁻⁸⁹). Recapture of rare ASVs (1–3 reads) showed the same pattern (0.48 within library vs 0.19 across): library preparation roughly doubles the technical dissimilarity contributed by sequencing alone, with a proportionally larger effect on presence–absence than on relative abundances. Sequencing run was accordingly modelled as nested within library preparation; because each sample is present in every batch, technical and biological factors are crossed rather than confounded, and no batch correction was applied.")
DEL += [92]
NEW[93] = ("Relative to the biological signal, this technical variation is negligible. With the technical terms entered first, library preparation explained 0.03 % of the Bray–Curtis variance over the 1,784 rarefied samples (R² = 0.0003, p = 0.63) and sequencing run within library 0.005 % (p = 1.00), against 9.2 % for tissue and 37.7 % for fish identity (both p = 0.002); library preparation cannot be tested marginally, being aliased with run label. Mean Bray–Curtis dissimilarity was 0.13 between two sequencing runs of one library and 0.25 across libraries, against 0.88 between different fish of the same tissue and 0.94 across tissues.")
NEW[96] = ("Three witnesses separate this effect from the biology it could be mistaken for. Within a single genotypic category, where a column effect cannot be a category effect, it persists but more weakly: among P. toxostoma alone in 17 of 24 strata in both passes (ratio 1.65), among C. nasus alone in 6 (pass 1) and 7 (pass 2) of 24 (ratio 1.63); hybrids alone are too few to be tested, and the only single-category station (Chavannes-sur-Suran, 19 P. toxostoma) is too small for a separate witness. In the symmetric ordering with category fitted first, column still contributes (21 and 22 of 24 strata). And plate row is significant in 7 and 6 of 24 strata and plate edge in none, making the effect specific to column, as expected of an effect tracking processing order rather than evaporation or thermal gradients. The mechanism of the column effect is not identified: extraction order, reagent-batch drift within a plate, cross-contamination between adjacent wells and the delay between capture and dissection are not separated by these data, and no negative control isolates any of them.")
NEW[103] = ("Fitted in both orderings without plate position, genotypic category accounted for a median of 1.7 % to 2.8 % of compositional variance across the 48 metric × tissue × run strata in pass 1 and 1.4 % to 3.0 % in pass 2, against a median of 19 % and 18 % for station fitted after category (range across strata 14–36 % in pass 1 and 14–35 % in pass 2) and of 20.9 % and 20.1 % for station fitted first (Fig. 3c; Supplementary Table S9). No tissue is detected by all four metrics in all three runs under both permutation schemes, in either pass, with or without plate position (Fig. 3a, b), so a global statement that hybrids differ is not supportable. One result is robust to every choice made here — both passes, with and without plate position, all three runs and both permutation schemes: the midgut on Jaccard (p ≤ 0.044). The caudal fin on weighted UniFrac is detected in all three runs and both schemes in pass 1 (p = 0.007–0.024), with or without plate position, and in no run in pass 2 (p = 0.073–0.235). Removing 22 randomly chosen individuals from the 158 that are not introgressed, twenty times, never restored the caudal signal in pass 2, which attributes this displacement to the definition of hybrids rather than to sample size. At 500 reads the caudal signal is partial in pass 1 (three runs sequentially, p = 0.022–0.049; one of three with station fixed by blocks) and absent in pass 2; restricted to the samples retained at 3,000 reads, the 500-read matrices give the same result as at 3,000 reads (all three runs, both station-blocked schemes, p = 0.010–0.026), so that the attenuation reflects dilution by the caudal-fin samples that pass the threshold only at 500 reads, not depth. As at 3,000 reads, none of twenty random removals of 22 non-introgressed individuals from pass 2 reproduced the pass-1 pattern at 500 reads. The hindgut is the weakest tissue in pass 1 (only Jaccard is significant, in one run of three with station fixed by blocks) but in pass 2 all four metrics detect it in all three runs with station fixed by blocks; the witness does not resolve whether this difference reflects sample size or the definition of hybrids. The principal and sensitivity models differ in 7 of the 16 metric × tissue cells in each pass, of which 5 (pass 1) and 3 (pass 2) move between robust detection (three runs, both schemes), partial detection and none. The separable sub-design detects category in 3 of 48 tests in pass 1 and 1 of 48 in pass 2 (with plate position), and Rosières, in pass 2, detects it in the gill on Jaccard and unweighted UniFrac in all three runs (p = 0.010–0.030).")
NEW[104] = ("Whether the loss of the caudal-fin signal between passes is produced by dilution of the hybrid–P. toxostoma contrast by the 19 near-parental hybrids of P. toxostoma background was tested (§8.12) and remains undetermined. Relabelling as hybrids 19 P. toxostoma and 3 C. nasus drawn from the stations of the near-parental individuals removed the pass-1 signal in 16 of 20 draws, so that such dilution is sufficient to produce the displacement; but the near-parental hybrids of P. toxostoma background differed neither from pure P. toxostoma (p = 0.28–0.60) nor from intermediate hybrids (p = 0.15–0.26; n = 25–26), so that dilution is not established.")
NEW[107] = ("Because PERMANOVA is sensitive to heterogeneous dispersion, this bears on the location results. With station fixed by blocks, dispersion in the caudal fin was homogeneous on all four metrics in pass 2 and in all but one test in pass 1 (weighted UniFrac, one run of three, p = 0.048, hybrids most dispersed), so that the caudal-fin location signal on weighted UniFrac in pass 1 is a difference in composition in two runs and cannot be separated from a dispersion difference in the third. The robust midgut signal on Jaccard was accompanied by homogeneous dispersion in pass 1 and in two runs of three in pass 2. By contrast, dispersion was heterogeneous in all three runs in the midgut on unweighted UniFrac in both passes and, in pass 2, in the midgut and hindgut on Bray–Curtis, P. toxostoma being the most dispersed category there; the location signals on those metrics in those compartments cannot be separated from a dispersion effect. Because betadisper accepts no covariate, the plate-position effect is not adjusted for, and a category whose samples are more spread across plate columns could carry inflated dispersion. Supplementary Table S10 and Figure S3.")

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- Discussion
NEW[112] = ("Across alpha diversity, community dispersion and the 4H index, the microbiota of hybrids between C. nasus and P. toxostoma showed no transgressive pattern. Where hybrids could be placed relative to their parents, they fell within the parental range: dominance of C. nasus in caudal-fin alpha diversity in pass 1, a parental, Intersection-dominated 4H index in the gill, and preferential retention of the core taxa shared by both parental species in every compartment. The departures detected in composition were small — genotypic category accounted for a median of 1.4–3.0 % of compositional variance against about a fifth for station — and confined to particular compartments, metrics and definitions of a hybrid. This places the system with natural hybrid zones in which the environmental component dominates [23, 24] rather than with those in which host ancestry does [21, 22], while keeping in view that a small genetic effect is the expected magnitude for a heritable but weakly heritable trait [9], detectable only once the spatial and technical axes have been separated [10].")
NEW[113] = ("The expectation derived from Guivier et al. [31], that a hybrid signal would be most pronounced in the digestive compartments, is partly supported. The only compositional result that withstood every analytical choice concerned the midgut, on a presence-based metric, and in pass 2 both digestive compartments were detected by all four metrics with station fixed by blocks. But the caudal fin carried, in pass 1, a signal on weighted UniFrac that no digestive compartment showed on that metric, the gill was the compartment in which the 4H index was robustly parental, and the 4H index distinguished no pair of compartments. Hybrid status therefore does not matter equally in all compartments, but which compartment carries a difference depends on the metric. Requiring both an abundance-weighted and a presence-based metric makes this dependence explicit rather than resolving it; it also accounts for the 4H index, a presence-based construct in its principal form, departing from its abundance-based version in every compartment but the gill.")
NEW[114] = ("How hybrids are defined mattered as much as which compartment is examined. The caudal-fin signal obtained with the 20 intermediate hybrids disappeared when the 22 near-parental individuals were added, and random removals of 22 non-introgressed fish never reproduced the displacement, which is therefore attributable to who is called a hybrid rather than to sample size; whether near-parental hybrids of P. toxostoma background dilute the contrast because their microbiota resembles that of P. toxostoma could not be established at the available sample size. In a mosaic hybrid zone [30] in which most introgressed individuals depart from a parental genome on a single chromosome, a hybrid class is a decision, and we suggest that studies of natural hybrid zones report their results under both definitions.")
NEW[115] = ("That hybrids were not more dispersed than their parents runs against the expectation that genomes combining parental elements in variable proportions carry more variable microbiota. The opposite tendency, hybrids less dispersed than both parents, was clear only in pass 2 with station fixed by blocks, absent within stations and carried mainly by Bray–Curtis, and should not be read as a general property of hybrids here. The analysis also illustrates a pitfall of unbalanced designs: without correction for group size, the distance to the group centre is underestimated in small groups, and because hybrids were the smallest category in every stratum, the uncorrected analysis yielded a strong and apparently replicated reduction of hybrid dispersion — 32 of 48 station-blocked tests in pass 1 and 45 of 68 within stations — that largely disappeared once corrected. Hybrid classes are small in most natural hybrid zones, and the correction should be applied by default in that setting [48].")
NEW[116] = ("The 4H index adds two points. First, in its abundance-based version — the form in which the framework's authors compared published systems [14] — the index placed every compartment on the parental axis (Gain + Loss = 0.22–0.35), as in the Neotoma woodrat system (0.12) and unlike the Aspidoscelis lizard and Kikihia cicada systems (0.71 and 0.64); since transgression has been associated with ecological success rather than with dysbiosis in a hybrid lizard [15], its absence here carries no implication for hybrid fitness either way. Second, as its authors found for a natural hybrid system, the index was sensitive to its settings: the choice between presence and abundance reversed the reading in three compartments of four, and in the caudal fin so did the number of hosts per class, from 9 to 26–27. A null hybrid built from parental individuals already sits close to the 0.5 boundary between the two axes, which makes that boundary a weak criterion for calling a hybrid microbiota transgressive. We therefore read the 4H index as a description of taxon membership conditional on its settings, not as an independent test of the intermediate, dominant and transgressive hypotheses.")
NEW[117] = ("Four limits bound these conclusions. Genotypic category is confounded with station, and the three stations carrying all three categories hold 65–74 individuals, so that category effects are estimated mostly between stations. Plate position affected composition and followed processing order in every campaign; its mechanism — extraction order, reagent drift within a plate, cross-contamination between adjacent wells or time spent in the holding tank — is not identified, and because C. nasus was generally processed first it could contribute to the dominance of C. nasus in caudal-fin alpha diversity, which was not tested. Rarefaction to 3,000 reads retained intermediate hybrids less often than P. toxostoma in the caudal fin, a selection that is declared but cannot be removed for presence-based metrics. And only 64 % of reads could be aggregated to genus for the 4H index, whose genus-level reading therefore rests on the better-assigned part of each community.")

